Diagnosed CSRF token regeneration issue caused by multiple PHP file includes.
Found out that every time a PHP file was loaded (included), it was making a brand new security token — so the old one stopped working.
Fixed CSRF token mismatch by generating the token only once per session.
Fixed it by telling the code: "only make a new token if one doesn't already exist." This way it stays the same the whole time the user is on the site.
Identified repeated session_start() and token regeneration across layout files.
Found that the "start session" command was being called again and again in different layout files, and each time it was creating a new token by mistake.
Debugged Promise-based file upload failures returning HTTP 419 (CSRF mismatch).
Looked into why file uploads (using JavaScript Promises like fetch) were failing with error 419, which means the security token didn't match.
Investigated HTTP 422 validation errors during AJAX file uploads.
Checked why some uploads were failing with error 422 — this means the file or form data didn't pass Laravel's validation rules (like wrong file type or missing field).
Resolved missing CSRF token in fetch()/FormData upload requests.
Fixed the issue where the security token wasn't being sent along with the file upload request, so the server didn't know the request was safe.
Verified correct CSRF meta tag placement in Blade layouts.
Checked that the special HTML tag holding the token (<meta name="csrf-token">) was properly placed in every page so JavaScript could read it correctly.
Corrected improper fetch() headers for multipart file uploads.
Fixed a mistake where the code was manually setting a header that it shouldn't — this was breaking how file uploads are sent to the server.
Diagnosed stale CSRF tokens caused by layout caching and partial views.
Found that sometimes an old (outdated) token was being shown to users because parts of the page were cached (saved and reused) instead of freshly loaded.
Investigated upload_max_filesize and post_max_size configuration causing empty POST requests.
Checked PHP settings that control the maximum file size allowed — if a file was too big, the whole form data (including the token) would get wiped out, causing errors.
Verified session cookie transmission in AJAX requests to prevent CSRF failures.
Made sure that the browser was properly sending the session cookie with each request, since the token check depends on that cookie being present.
Used browser DevTools and Laravel logs to trace request, validation, and token issues.
Used the browser's built-in inspector tool and Laravel's error logs to find exactly where and why requests were failing.
Debugged Promise rejection handling for file upload requests.
Fixed the JavaScript code so that when an upload failed, the error was properly caught and shown instead of failing silently.
Ensured proper script loading order for Axios/JavaScript CSRF configuration.
Made sure the JavaScript code that sets up the token (for Axios) runs before any file upload happens — otherwise the token wouldn't be ready in time.
